A||||3||Formula88||Vortec Heads?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||12-21-2001||06:49 PM||||Ok, so I'm reading that there's now a TPI manifold for Vortec heads.  My question is:  Are the Aluminum Fast Burn heads and the Iron Vortec heads the exact same head, except for material?  The article in a magazine I picked up today was talking about the manifold, but went out of their way to mention IRON Vortec heads, not just 'Vortec' heads.  Are they interchangeable?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Will||12-21-2001||07:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>Ok, so I'm reading that there's now a TPI manifold for Vortec heads.  My question is:  Are the Aluminum Fast Burn heads and the Iron Vortec heads the exact same head, except for material?  The article in a magazine I picked up today was talking about the manifold, but went out of their way to mention IRON Vortec heads, not just 'Vortec' heads.  Are they interchangeable?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What magazine? I'd like to read about that. TPI is awesome; all the low end grunt you could ever want! We have a '74 XJ6 with a TPI 400. That 4000 lbs car will spin tires on dry pavement with just a bit of provocation, no power braking necessary.<P>The factory Vortec heads are iron. Someone (Edelbrock?) has made aluminum copies of the iron Vortec heads, with minor changes (hopefully improvments).||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||12-21-2001||07:08 PM||||The URL for info on the TPI manifold for the Vortec/Fastburn Heads is:  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.sdpc2000.com/cart.asp?action=prod_detail&catid=1460&pid=13740" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sdpc2000.com/cart.asp?action=prod_detail&catid=1460&pid=13740</A> <P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000003||BV MotorSports||12-21-2001||09:14 PM||||First off the Iron Vortec heads are the best flowing "as cast" iron head GM has EVER made. In a recent test by CHP the iron Vortecs were up against all the major after market heads. Even though they had the smallest valves of the bunch (194's) they ranked #4 in flow. Thats a set of $450 heads against heads costing almost 2k! Oh, did I mention the top 3 were aluminum heads? Like the Brodix Track 1 and AFR 220's!<P>BUT!<BR>I wouldn't go the "drilled and tapped for old-style intakes" route with <BR>the<BR>Vortecs.  Whether they bolt up or not, the ports don't match very well.  <BR>In<BR>fact, some have had problems with vaccuum leaks, they match up so <BR>poorly.<BR>Either use a vortec intake, or go another route.  I'm running a <BR>Performer<BR>RPM Vortec intake with the Vortecs on mine (heads about $420 and intake<BR>about $180).  And even though many are running .480 lift without<BR>modification, GM doesn't recommend it, and when I checked mine the <BR>valve<BR>retainers interfered - by about .015 to .020 inches.  They were pushing <BR>on<BR>the seals.  So I needed the guides cut down.  I'm also running steel <BR>shim<BR>gaskets to keep the quench tight (.035-.039), and the head surfaces <BR>were<BR>kinda sloppy (about .004-.006 out of flat - too much for a .019 steel<BR>gasket), and my machinist also recommended re-grinding the valves - <BR>said<BR>the factory machining tended to be rough and sloppy (and you could see <BR>it).<BR>So I spent a little over $200 (I think that's all it was) on my brand <BR>new<BR>heads.  In other words, about $850, including the added machining and <BR>the<BR>required intake.  So they begin to look like not so great a bargain.  <BR>On<BR>the other hand, anything probably would've needed milling (the surface<BR>would likely have been too rough, at least),  and I'd have needed to <BR>spend<BR>at least $120 on an intake anyway.  I think the only thing you could<BR>realistically get (off the shelf, anyway) for the same money would be <BR>World<BR>Products SR Torquers - which won't flow with the Vortecs (off the <BR>shelf,<BR>anyway).  Though, for two or three hundred more I could've gone the<BR>standard intake route and gotten Trick Flows or Edelbrocks, with guide<BR>plates, screwed-in studs, etc.<P>I don't know anyone running the vortecs that isn't happy with the<BR>performance, though.  Run vortecs, a Performer RPM intake, and a CC <BR>XE268H<BR>and you're talking in the neighborhood of 400+ lb.-ft. of stump-pulling<BR>torque from about 2500 rpm to about 5000 or 5500 rpm.  An XE262 is <BR>supposed<BR>to work very nicely as well, and might be less likely to require <BR>machining<BR>down the guides for retainer to seal clearance.  If you're going to <BR>stick<BR>with a decent street cam (and I've looked at trying to boost the top <BR>end a<BR>little bit with a hotter cam - you lose too much at the bottom - it <BR>doesn't<BR>pay - at least for a street engine) - the vortecs work pretty nicely<BR>(though they add a few lbs. vs. an aluminum head).  AFR's will give you<BR>better high end performance, but they're much spendier, and you better <BR>plan<BR>on revving to 7000, and fine tuning your gearing accordingly to make <BR>them<BR>pay off.  For solid street performance the Vortecs and an XE262 or 268 <BR>are<BR>tough to beat.  But there are lots of ways to go, and if you can get<BR>cheaper used stuff, you might find a combo that's better.<P>Pro Action (made by AFR) make a really nice head that is designed like the vortec and flows 230cfm just like the vortecs but uses the common pre '87 intake bolt pattern. They will cost you about $499.<P>As for your other question the iron Vortecs p/n # 12558060 have 1.94/1.50 valve and have small 64cc chambers.<BR>The Aluminum "fast burn" (same thing as Vortec) p/n 12464298 have raised runners D shaped exhaust ports and have 62cc chambers. They use slightly larger valves 2.00/1.55 and are rated for up to 500 out of the box.<BR>They will run you about 1,200 or so.<P>Let me know if you have any more questions.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
